---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLivePlans, getLastUpdated } from '../lib/data';
import { formatDate } from '../lib/format';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAME, SITE_URL } from '../lib/site';
import { withUtm } from '../lib/utm';

const plans = getLivePlans();
const lastUpdated = getLastUpdated();

const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Methodology',
  description: 'How VM Compare South Africa collects, normalises and ranks VPS prices.',
  url: `${SITE_URL}/methodology/`,
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
  dateModified: lastUpdated,
};
---

<Base
  title="Methodology — how we compare VPS prices in South Africa"
  description="How VM Compare collects VPS prices, converts them to VAT-inclusive rand, ignores promotional teasers and ranks plans by resource value."
  canonicalPath="/methodology/"
  jsonLd={structuredData}
>
  <section class="provider-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">Methodology</span>
      <h1>How we compare VPS prices</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">
        The goal is simple: make it possible to compare a R199 plan from one provider with a R419
        plan from another without doing VAT maths or decoding marketing copy. Here is exactly how
        we do it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        For every plan we record the provider, plan name, vCPU count, RAM, storage size and type
        (NVMe, SSD or HDD), bandwidth, monthly price, billing term, setup fee, IPv4 address count,
        location and the features the provider advertises. We also keep the date each plan was last
        checked and a price-history snapshot per provider.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current dataset contains <strong>{plans.length} live plans</strong> across the providers
        listed on the <a href="/providers/">providers page</a>. The last successful check was
        {formatDate(lastUpdated)}.
      </p>

      <h2>VAT normalisation</h2>
      <p>
        South African providers are inconsistent about VAT. Some advertise prices including VAT,
        some excluding it, and some do not say. We convert every price to <strong>VAT-inclusive
        rand</strong> at the standard 15% rate:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>If the provider says the price includes VAT, we leave it unchanged.</li>
        <li>If the provider says the price excludes VAT, we multiply by 1.15.</li>
        <li>If the provider does not state a treatment, we assume the price excludes VAT, multiply by 1.15 and mark the row with an asterisk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The price exactly as advertised is always stored and shown underneath the comparison price,
        so you can check our arithmetic against the provider's page.
      </p>

      <h2>Promotions and billing terms</h2>
      <p>
        A first-month discount is not a price. We ignore introductory promotions, limited-time sales
        and free-domain incentives, and use the recurring monthly price instead. Where a provider
        only sells on an annual commitment, we show the effective monthly price and note the
        commitment in the plan row.
      </p>

      <h2>How the ranking works</h2>
      <p>
        The main table is sorted by absolute monthly price by default. That answers the simplest
        question: which plan costs the least? You can re-sort by <strong>price per GB of RAM</strong>
        to see which plan gives the most memory for your money, or filter by vCPU, storage type and
        provider.
      </p>
      <p>
        The category cards on the homepage find the cheapest plan with <em>at least</em> the stated
        RAM, so a 6 GB plan can win the 4 GB category if it is cheaper. We also calculate the best
        price per GB in each category, because the cheapest sticker price is not always the best
        value.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Price is not the whole story.</strong> A plan with NVMe storage, included backups,
        local support and a Cape Town or Johannesburg location may be worth paying more for. Those
        details are shown as feature badges and in the provider notes, not folded into a single
        opaque score.
      </div>

      <h2>Freshness and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Prices are checked automatically every week. If a provider page cannot be read, we keep the
        last known price and mark it as stale rather than deleting the plan. If you spot a price
        that is wrong or out of date, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
        with the provider and plan name and we will correct it.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>
        VM Compare is an independent comparison. We do not accept payment for placement or for a
        better rank. If affiliate links are added in future, they will be disclosed and will not
        affect the ranking. The site is a companion to the
        <a href={withUtm('https://number1.co.za')} rel="noopener">number1.co.za</a> blog posts on South African
        VPS pricing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
